llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
141; needs 116 and 140): with hot experts, the add of the device's and the
CPU's expert outputs runs inside the fused MoE tail, and the lookup of the
CPU's expert ids runs inside the fused top-k kernel.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
A Qwen3.6 35B-A3B token with hot experts (patch 140) hands every layer to
the CPU and back. The device's work from the CPU's return to the next
hand-off is on the critical path: the add of the two expert halves, the
MoE tail, the attention or DeltaNet block, the router, top-k, and the
lookup of the ids the CPU runs (a GET_ROWS of the layer's cold-id table).
On an RX 590 each dispatch there costs about 5.4 us of idle time before
the next dependent kernel starts, besides the kernel's own run time. The
add (about 6 us) and the lookup (about 5 us) are two such dispatches per
layer, 80 per token, and each does almost no work.

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-server from the Ollama image, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B
Q4_K_M, hot experts with --moe-hot-mib 4096, ten chat prompts, 400 greedy
tokens each, two runs per build): 37.76 -> 38.38 tok/s (+1.6%), and every
answer byte-identical to the build without this patch. Under rocprof the
token issues 928 instead of 1,007 dispatches; the fused tail takes 15.3
instead of 11.7 us and the fused top-k 14.5 instead of 12.1 us, against
the 6 and 5 us kernels and two dispatch gaps they replace.

WHAT
----
- moe_tail_rms_norm_mul_q8_1_f32 gains a second experts input (a template
  flag, so the one-input kernel is unchanged) and adds it element-wise
  before weighting. ggml_cuda_match_moe_tail_add matches, at an ADD of two
  same-shape f32 tensors, a weighted reduction and MoE tail (patch 116)
  whose experts are that ADD, and checks the whole group with
  ggml_can_fuse_subgraph; h must not overlap either half, since it is
  written while they are read. graph_optimize keeps both halves allocated
  until the tail's last node.
- topk_moe_cuda gains an optional table and output: each selected id is
  also looked up in the table (I32, one value per expert). The lookups are
  issued after the selection loop, from the lane that holds each selected
  expert, so their latency is paid once. The matcher accepts, right after
  the top-k group, a RESHAPE of the ids and a GET_ROWS of such a table by
  it, for one token; the RESHAPE stays an output because the hot experts'
  lookup reads it too.

Numerics are unchanged: the add is the same float add of the same two
values, and the lookup copies integers.

